==Old English==

===Etymology===
From {{inh|ang|gem-pro|*fimf}}, from {{etyl|ine-pro|ang}} {{m|ine-pro|*pénkʷe||five}}. Cognates include Old High German {{m|goh|fimf}}, ''finf'' (German ''[[fünf]]''), Old Saxon {{m|osx|fīf}}, Old Dutch ''fīf'', {{m|odt|vīf}} (Dutch [[vijf]]), Old Norse ''[[fimm]]'' (Swedish ''[[fem]]''), Gothic {{m|got|𐍆𐌹𐌼𐍆}}. The Indo-European root is also the source of Greek {{m|el|πέντε}}, Latin ''[[quinque]]'', Sanskrit {{m|sa|पञ्चन्}}.

===Pronunciation===
* {{IPA|/fiːf/|lang=ang}}

===Numeral===
